# NOTES #

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_J._Van_de_Graaff#cite_ref-2]  Despite variant spellings in some authorities, the subject published only as "Van de Graaff." This Americanized form (capital "Van," double "f") differs from Dutch conventions. The  [LINK: (AIP style guide) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AIP_style]  uses "Van de Graaff", as do obituaries. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_J._Van_de_Graaff#cite_note-1]

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_J._Van_de_Graaff#cite_ref-18]  Van de Graaff later confided to colleagues that he had wanted to include in the letter his belief that useful amounts of nuclear energy might be liberated by the disintegration of uranium or thorium, but "felt Compton would think this too bold." [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_J._Van_de_Graaff#cite_note-FOOTNOTERose19682-17]

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_J._Van_de_Graaff#cite_ref-68]  Although Van de Graaff's patent uses "insulating," the device is also commonly called the "insulated core transformer"; both terms appear in the technical literature.


